## Event page format and retrieval

Event pages are HTML. Request the clean, parameter-free event URL and read the response
as returned.

superbet.ro serves prerendered HTML to recognised non-browser clients. That response
contains the event title, meta description, canonical URL, hreflang alternates, the
`robots` `unavailable_after` directive, team names, kick-off time, market names and odds,
with no JavaScript execution required.

Delivery depends on client identification. A browser-like client instead receives a small
client-rendered application shell: a generic title, no description, no canonical, and a
body containing only a `noscript` element and an empty container. If the response matches
that description you have the shell and not the page. Say that the content could not be
retrieved, and do not report the event as non-existent or unavailable on that basis.

## Interpreting event information and odds

- Odds use decimal format by default.
- Displayed event times can vary by location. Preserve the timezone or UTC offset supplied
  in the response. If neither is provided, state that the timezone is unspecified rather
  than assuming Romanian local time.
- Prerendered responses may be cached and the refresh interval is unspecified. Odds may
  have changed since the response was generated. Do not describe retrieved prices as
  real-time.
- For in-play markets and the latest available odds, use the event page or the relevant
  live hub rather than a previously retrieved response.
- Do not present a match shown as suspended as currently available for betting.
- Preserve the exact market and selection labels when quoting odds. Enhanced odds and other
  special markets may differ from the standard match-result market.
- Market descriptions may omit whether extra time or penalties are included. Consult the
  relevant market description, betting rules and extra-time guide rather than inferring
  settlement terms.
- Include the clean event URL with quoted odds so users can check current prices and
  availability. If a requested market is missing, state that it was not found in the
  retrieved response; do not invent a price.

## Event pages and discovery

Individual event pages are under `https://superbet.ro/cote/`.

- A football event URL follows `https://superbet.ro/cote/fotbal/{event-slug}-{eventId}`.
- The numeric event ID identifies one specific fixture and is shared across Superbet
  markets. The same fixture on superbet.pl, superbet.rs, superbet.bet.br and superbet.gr
  carries the same ID with a localised slug, and each event page declares the others
  through hreflang.
- To answer a question about another market, follow the hreflang URL declared on the page.
  Do not build a cross-market URL by swapping the domain: the slug is localised, and a
  fixture offered in one market may not be offered in another.
- Do not reuse an ID taken from an earlier meeting between the same teams. Discover the
  current event URL from the sports hub or the events sitemap instead.
- Discover current URLs through links in the relevant sports hub HTML or the
  [events sitemap](https://superbet.ro/sitemap/events.xml).
- For football, use the [football hub](https://superbet.ro/pariuri-sportive/fotbal)
  and [live football hub](https://superbet.ro/pariuri-sportive/fotbal/live).
- Use the clean URL without query parameters when requesting or citing an event.

## Event availability and fallback guidance

- Event pages remain available until 24 hours after scheduled kick-off.
- The `robots: unavailable_after` timestamp marks this expiry. It does not
  represent the actual match finish time or guarantee that betting remains available.
- Event URLs are not permanent historical records. After expiry an event URL should not be
  treated as a source of current or historical odds.
- After a match finishes, do not present remaining cached odds as currently
  available for betting.
- For active matches, use the relevant sport's live hub or the
  [all-sports live hub](https://superbet.ro/pariuri-sportive/live).
- A request for a path that does not exist may still return HTTP 200 with an application
  shell rather than an error status. Do not treat a 200 response as confirmation that an
  event or game exists. Confirm existence by finding the URL in a hub or sitemap.
- If an event cannot be accessed or returns no usable content, explain that current odds
  could not be verified. Do not infer prices or availability from older responses
  or sitemap entries.

